One of the most common errors that you can have if you're writing your =init.el= through org-mode is that you forget to close parentheses when you're writing between source code blocks. If you've forgotten some parentheses, this will produce the =End of file during parsing= error. This can be easily checked in the =init.el= file that was tangled using =M-x check-parens RET=.[fn:3]

You can also enable =show-paren-mode=, which will help

* Structure of the Emacs config

- =early-init.el= :: Optimisations for starting up Emacs

- =init.el= :: Contains only the foundations for the functioning of our config. Also, it contains Lisp macros. It will load the different configuration modules that we've defined.

- =emacs-modules/= directory :: These are the configuration modules. They don't introduce new bits of Emacs Lisp code, instead they just tweak some configuration for packages.

- =custom-lisp-functions/= directory :: This actually loads custom Emacs Lisp code.

- =literate-config.org= :: is the current file, which describes the entire architecture.

* Managing the literate configuration
** How the configuration is created

The configuration files (e.g. =init.el= and the modules) will be created upon acting on the =org-babel-tangle= function.

When tangling, the process will create these files:
- =~/emacs-literate/init.el=
- =~/emacs-literate/early-init.el=

These files can either be "manually" copied to the =~/.emacs.d= directory, or through =Chemacs2=, the literate config can be added as an alternative profile

See also =org-babel-execute-subtree= for the process in which =org-babel= actually works.

** Making edits to the configuration as needed

When wanting to make changes, you have to go into =literate-configuration.org= specifically, and then use =C-c C-v C-t= (=M-x org-babel-tangle=) if you want to propagate the changes to the files. The "direction" where each file will be sent is thankfully part of the org source block syntax.

